section: profiles
title: Profiles and packs
about: |
  The profiles of the entity: their order, their versions, their templates, and their variables.
rules:
- id: profile-order
  title: The order of the add-on profiles means nothing
  engines:
  - all
  tier: static
  severity: note
  status: seen
  when: (.profiles | length) > 0
  what: |
    Palette stores the profiles in the order it receives, and the provider sends a set, so two
    clusters from one configuration get two orders. The install order on the cluster is the pack
    priority, not the profile order. Never compare the raw order. This note names an entity with
    more than one add-on profile, where a reader can assume an order.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      [ .profiles | map(select((.source.type // "") == "add-on"))
        | select(length > 1)
        | "the entity names \(length) add-on profiles (\(map(.source.name) | join(", "))): their order means nothing, and the install order is the pack priority" ]
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Sort the profiles by a stated key in the API; the provider keeps the configured order.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
    note: the provider discards the configured order
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
    note: the install order on the cluster does not depend on the profile order
  - kind: source
    note: the profiles are a set since v0.28.1
  - kind: source
    note: Palette orders the packs of the add-on profiles by the priority of each pack, not by the order of the profiles
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/two-addons.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/full-records.json
- id: position-zero
  title: The profile at position 0 is the infrastructure profile
  engines:
  - all
  tier: static
  severity: warn
  status: source
  when: (.profiles | length) > 0 and (.entity.spec.profiles | length) > 0
  what: |
    Some readers of a cluster read the profile at position 0 as the infrastructure profile. The
    console creates each cluster with an add-on at position 0, and an upgrade of the
    infrastructure profile puts that profile last. Each reader then reads the wrong profile, in
    one case only. The FIPS guard of the console matters in a FIPS tenant, and the
    `clusterProfileTemplate` field of the dashboard API matters to a client that reads it. The
    repave check of CNI and CSI matters when an add-on profile holds a CNI or a CSI pack. The
    check reads the record of the first profile of the entity, whose `type` must be `cluster` or
    `infra`.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      def addon: (.source.type // "") as $t | $t != "cluster" and $t != "infra";
      .clusterUid as $cluster
      | .entity.spec.profiles[0].uid as $first
      | [ .profiles[] | select(addon) | .source.name as $p | (.packs // [])[]
          | select(.layer == "cni" or .layer == "csi") | "the \(.layer) pack \(.name) of \($p)" ] as $layers
      | [ .profiles[] | select(.source.uid == $first) | select(addon) | .source.name as $p
          | "profile \($p) at position 0 is of type \(.source.type // "unknown"), not cluster or infra: a reader of position 0 reads \($p) as the infrastructure profile",
            "the FIPS guard of the console reads it in a FIPS tenant only",
            "only a client that reads the field clusterProfileTemplate of the dashboard API gets \($p)",
            (if ($layers | length) > 0
              then "the repave check of CNI and CSI can read \($layers | join(", "))"
              else "no add-on profile holds a CNI or a CSI pack, so the repave check of CNI and CSI is right" end),
            (if $cluster == null
              then "put the infrastructure profile at position 0 of spec.profiles of the create: it stays there until a new version of it goes to the end of the stored order"
              else ("a PUT of the profiles in another order keeps the stored order of the cluster, and a new version of the infrastructure profile goes to the end",
                    "read the infrastructure profile by its type in spec.clusterProfileTemplates, and the FIPS mode in status.fips.mode") end) ]
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Read the infrastructure profile by its type, not by its position.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
    note: the FIPS guard of the console passes when position 0 is an add-on; reproduced against FIPS fixtures
  - kind: source
    note: the position-0 reads and the first-match pack lookups
  - kind: source
    note: the console of Palette guards the FIPS choice when it shows the details of a cluster
  - kind: source
    note: an added profile and a new version of the infrastructure profile go to the end of the stored order, and the summary gives position 0 as clusterProfileTemplate
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/addon-first.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/full-records.json
- id: edit-in-place
  title: A published profile version stays as published
  engines:
  - all
  tier: race
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  what: |
    The API takes an edit of a published version: the version keeps its uid and its number, stays
    published, and no new version appears. The `immutable-clusterprofiles` flag of the provider
    stops the edit through the provider alone. The API, the console, and a push again of a pack
    change the version whatever the flag says. No check before the build finds an edit. `strata
    verify` on a record from before reports `DRIFT`, and the audit log names who changed it.
  reproduce:
  - strata verify <record.json> --who
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Lock a published version on the server, as a setting.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: strata live example
    note: a PUT of a published version is accepted, and the version keeps its uid and its number
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/profiles/cluster-profiles/modify-cluster-profiles/update-cluster-profile/
  - kind: source
    note: the immutable-clusterprofiles feature_preview flag is a check of the provider
